Tangible and intangible assets are recognised at historical cost.Where applicable, they are stated at amortised cost or historical cost.Assets are depreciated or amortised on a straight-line basis over their expected useful life.Where applicable, residual values are taken into account when calculating depreciation on tangible assets.For intangible assets, residual values are only taken into account insofar as a third party is obligated to assume the intangible asset at the end of its useful life.Furthermore, residual values are taken into account when there is an active market for the intangible asset, the residual value can be established in reference to said market and the active market is expected to exist at the end of the useful life of the intangible asset.Whenever possible, the recognition of non-current intangible assets is broken down into a research and a development phase.Expenditure in the research phase must always be recognised as expenses.Expenditure in the development phase is to be capitalised from the date on which the following can be satisfied:
